    <body>
      <sec id="article-26730.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Patellar tendon rupture is a relatively uncommon but functionally disabling disruption of the knee extensor mechanism, typically occurring at the junction between the inferior patellar pole and tibial tubercle. The condition most often affects men aged 30&#x000a0;to 40 and arises when structurally compromised tendons are subjected to sudden high tensile loads. Predisposing factors include chronic tendinopathy, systemic conditions such as diabetes or chronic kidney disease, and exposure to corticosteroids or fluoroquinolones. The pathophysiology involves loss of continuity of the extensor apparatus, resulting in impaired knee extension and extensor lag. Clinical presentation includes acute pain, swelling, palpable gap, inability to extend the knee actively, and difficulty ambulating.</p>
        <p>Diagnosis relies on history and physical examination, supported by imaging studies,&#x000a0;including ultrasound or magnetic resonance imaging, to confirm tendon disruption and rule out intraarticular injury. Management typically involves acute primary repair or, in chronic cases, reconstructive techniques with possible augmentation. Complications may include rerupture, stiffness, or persistent weakness.&#x000a0;Prognosis depends on the timeliness of repair, the quality of reconstruction, and adherence to postoperative rehabilitation.</p>
        <p>This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to sharpen learners' skills in evaluating and managing patellar tendon rupture. Participants will deepen their understanding of the condition's etiology, risk factors, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, and best diagnostic and therapeutic practices.&#x000a0;Greater competence will empower clinicians to collaborate successfully with interprofessional teams&#x000a0;caring for affected individuals.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Differentiate complete rupture, partial tear, and intraarticular knee injury using focused history, risk-factor assessment, and functional examination of the extensor mechanism.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Interpret imaging studies to confirm patellar tendon rupture and characterize tear features relevant to management, including patellar height, tear location, tissue quality, and extent.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply&#x000a0;evidence-informed, personalized strategies for managing&#x000a0;patellar tendon rupture and preventing its possible sequelae.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement postoperative rehabilitation and interprofessional care using protected early-motion principles, structured follow-up, and defined return-to-function milestones to optimize recovery and minimize complications.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=26730&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=26730">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Patellar tendon rupture is a relatively uncommon injury that disrupts the distal extensor mechanism, involving complete tendon discontinuity between the inferior patellar pole and the tibial tubercle. The condition predominantly affects men in the 3rd to 4th decade of life and typically arises when a biologically weakened tendon is subjected to high tensile loads.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r1">[1]</xref> Ruptures are classified as acute or chronic based on time from injury, a distinction that directly informs management and prognosis.</p>
        <p>The knee extensor mechanism is essential for ambulation, providing active extension and resisting flexion.&#x000a0;Therefore, prompt diagnosis and restoration are critical to prevent profound functional limitation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r3">[3]</xref> Surgical management is determined by timing and tissue or tear characteristics: acute complete ruptures generally undergo primary repair, historically via transosseous tunnels, with suture anchors increasingly utilized. Reconstruction using autograft or allograft options may be indicated&#x000a0;for cases where primary repair is not feasible, particularly chronic tears or inadequate tissue quality.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Anatomy</bold>
</p>
        <p>The patellar tendon forms the distal component of the knee extensor mechanism, spanning from the inferior patellar pole to the tibial tubercle.&#x000a0;Although its bone-to-bone attachment classifies it as a ligament,&#x000a0;this fibrous band&#x000a0;is termed a "tendon" as it is the terminal portion of the quadriceps tendon prior to tibial insertion.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The extensor mechanism includes the quadriceps muscle group, quadriceps tendon, medial and lateral patellar retinacula, patella, patellar tendon, and tibial tubercle. The quadriceps comprises 4 muscles: rectus femoris (originating from the anterior superior iliac spine and superior acetabular rim), vastus lateralis (greater trochanter and lateral linea aspera), vastus intermedius (proximal femoral shaft), and vastus medialis (intertrochanteric line and medial linea aspera), which converge into the quadriceps tendon and insert onto the patella.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>The medial and lateral retinacula constitute fascial expansions continuous with the vastus fascia and function as secondary stabilizers, preserving partial active extension and straight-leg raise (SLR) capability despite disruption of the patellar or quadriceps tendon. The patella, a sesamoid bone, increases the extensor lever arm and enhances quadriceps efficiency.&#x000a0;Engagement with the trochlea begins at approximately 20&#x000b0; of flexion and completes by around 40&#x000b0;, while patellofemoral joint reaction forces reach approximately 3 times body weight during stair ascent and up to 7 times body weight during deep knee flexion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Structurally, the patellar tendon is broad proximally, reflecting the width of the inferior patella, and tapers distally as it inserts onto and invests the tibial tubercle. The mean tendon length and central width have been reported as 39 mm and 32 mm, respectively.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r6">[6]</xref> Tendon thickness ranges from 4.0 mm to 10.9 mm, with measurements exceeding 7.0 mm suggestive of tendinopathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Proximally, the deep surface lies adjacent to the patellar articular cartilage and becomes continuous anteriorly with the patellar periosteum. Distally, the tendon narrows and anchors the extensor mechanism to the tibia. Vascular supply derives predominantly from the inferior-lateral genicular artery, an anastomotic network connecting the inferior medial genicular artery and the anterior tibial recurrent artery, with additional contributions from the retropatellar fat of Hoffa. Relative hypovascularity at the proximal and distal ends, compared with the better-perfused midsubstance, corresponds with the most frequent sites of rupture.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Biomechanics</bold>
</p>
        <p>Regional variations in tendon loading and material behavior have been demonstrated. Biomechanical studies consistently show a flexion-dependent increase in tensile loading within the anterior patellar tendon, with relatively lower stresses posteriorly, resulting in higher peak and yield stresses in the anterior fibers compared with the posterior fibers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Sagittal-plane analyses indicate that the tendon&#x02019;s insertional regions experience strain approximately 3 to 4 times greater than the midsubstance, providing a plausible biomechanical explanation for the predominance of tears near osseous attachments. In structurally normal tendons, the load required to produce rupture has been estimated at approximately 17.5 times body weight, emphasizing that clinically observed failures typically reflect a combination of tensile overload and compromised tendon integrity.</p>
        <p>Mechanistically, rupture most often occurs during extensor mechanism overload with the knee in flexion against an eccentrically contracting quadriceps. In elite athletes, contributory kinematic patterns, including ankle plantarflexion and knee valgus, have also been described.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r10">[10]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Chronic inflammatory pathology, including patellar tendinopathy, compromises tendon structural integrity and increases susceptibility to rupture. Systemic medical conditions that impair tendon quality further weaken the tendon and predispose it to rupture.</p>
        <p>Multiple systemic and local factors have been associated with an elevated risk of patellar tendon rupture. Systemic conditions that may compromise tendon integrity include systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, diabetes mellitus, and chronic renal disease, particularly in patients undergoing renal dialysis. Medication-related risks include chronic systemic corticosteroid therapy, prior corticosteroid injections, and exposure to fluoroquinolone antibiotics. Local factors, such as prior injury, repetitive overuse, patellar tendinopathy, and degenerative changes within the tendon, further diminish tendon strength and increase rupture risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Disruption of the knee extensor mechanism can occur at multiple locations within the extensor chain. The 3 most common sites of disruption are the patella, quadriceps tendon, and patellar tendon.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r14">[14]</xref> Patellar fractures occur more than twice as frequently as tendon ruptures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r15">[15]</xref> Quadriceps tendon rupture is more common than patellar tendon rupture, particularly in adults older than 40.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>Contemporary epidemiologic data estimate the incidence of quadriceps tendon rupture at approximately 1.37 cases per 100,000 person-years and the incidence of patellar tendon rupture at approximately 0.68 per 100,000 person-years in the general population.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;Men are affected more frequently than women, a pattern attributed to greater muscular strength and consequent susceptibility to extensor mechanism injury. In contrast,&#x000a0;women may be partially protected by greater ligamentous laxity and hormonal variations associated with the menstrual cycle.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Patellar tendon rupture results from tensile overload of the extensor mechanism, often superimposed on chronic tendon degeneration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r18">[18]</xref> Rupture typically occurs during sudden quadriceps contraction with the knee in flexion, such as during stair ascent, landing from a jump, or abrupt directional changes while running. Tensile forces on the patellar tendon peak when the knee is flexed beyond 60&#x000b0;, explaining why most ruptures occur in this position. Ruptures can occur at 3 distinct locations. The most common site is proximal avulsion from the inferior patellar pole, with or without an osseous fragment. Strain at the tendon&#x02013;patella interface is 3 to 4 times higher than in the midsubstance of the tendon. Other potential rupture sites include midsubstance tears and tibial tubercle avulsions.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>
<bold>History</bold>
</p>
        <p>Acute patellar tendon rupture typically presents with an abrupt onset of infrapatellar pain and swelling following a discrete inciting event, usually involving sudden quadriceps contraction during loaded eccentric knee flexion, such as landing from a jump or misstepping on stairs. Patients often report an audible or palpable &#x0201c;pop,&#x0201d; a sensation of giving way, immediate functional instability, and difficulty with weight-bearing.</p>
        <p>History should systematically characterize symptom onset, pain location and severity, temporal progression, precipitating mechanism, aggravating and relieving factors, associated swelling or effusion, and the degree of functional limitation. Particular attention should be given to preinjury anterior knee pain suggestive of underlying tendinopathy, as well as systemic or medication-related factors that may compromise tendon integrity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>Injuries presenting beyond approximately 6 weeks from the index event are commonly considered chronic. Partial tears may evolve more insidiously and are frequently observed in athletes who continue activity in the setting of patellar tendinopathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Physical Examination</bold>
</p>
        <p>Physical examination should begin with inspection for soft-tissue swelling, ecchymosis, and tense effusion, as acute patellar tendon rupture is frequently associated with large hemarthrosis. Comparative assessment of patellar height is critical, as patella alta relative to the contralateral side may be apparent&#x000a0;(see <bold>Evaluation</bold>). Palpation typically reveals focal infrapatellar tenderness and, in complete ruptures, a palpable gap or defect just distal to the inferior patellar pole.</p>
        <p>The hallmark functional finding is disruption of the extensor mechanism. Active knee extension is markedly reduced due to pain and mechanical failure, and patients are often unable to perform or maintain an SLR. Preservation of medial and lateral retinacular tissues may allow partial active extension or SLR, often with residual extensor lag that can obscure diagnosis.</p>
        <p>Careful assessment is essential due to the prognostic impact of delayed recognition. Joint aspiration followed by local anesthetic injection may assist differentiation when examination is limited by pain or a large effusion, as true extensor mechanism disruption persists despite adequate analgesia.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Recommended radiographic evaluation includes anteroposterior and lateral views of the affected knee. In a complete rupture, radiographs may demonstrate patella alta (superiorly displaced patella). The Insall-Salvati ratio provides a rapid method to determine patella alta or baja (inferiorly displaced patella) on the lateral radiograph. This ratio is defined as the length of the patellar tendon divided by the length of the patella, ideally measured on a lateral view with the knee flexed to 30&#x000b0;. A normal ratio ranges from 0.8 to 1.2, with patella alta exceeding 1.2 and patella baja below 0.8. Radiographs may also reveal avulsion fractures or other concomitant knee injuries.</p>
        <p>Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is the most sensitive modality for diagnosing suspected patellar tendon rupture. MRI differentiates partial from complete rupture, identifies the precise rupture location, evaluates tendon degeneration, assesses patellar position, and detects concomitant intraarticular lesions. Ultrasound may serve as an adjunctive study, effectively detecting and localizing tendon disruption. Compared with MRI, ultrasound is less expensive and&#x000a0;may be more accessible, depending on equipment and operator expertise.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Partial-Thickness Ruptures</bold>
</p>
        <p>Partial-thickness patellar tendon ruptures are generally managed initially with nonoperative measures. Acute incomplete tears with a preserved extensor mechanism are typically treated with immobilization in full extension and protected weight-bearing, often using a hinged knee brace.</p>
        <p>Staged rehabilitation commonly begins with an early "acute" period (0&#x02013;14 days) focused on mitigating the adverse effects of immobilization through isometric quadriceps activation and stretching, followed by a combined rehabilitation and return-to-activity phase lasting approximately 3 to 6 months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Emphasis shifts toward restoring full mobility and advancing concentric and eccentric strengthening during rehabilitation, with gradual introduction of jogging as tolerated. The subsequent return-to-activity phase incorporates dynamic, sport-specific tasks to enhance tendon elasticity and neuromuscular coordination. Adjunctive interventions, including dry needling, percutaneous ultrasonic d&#x000e9;bridement, and extracorporeal shockwave therapy (ESWT), have been described.&#x000a0;Outcomes vary, and the supporting evidence remains heterogeneous.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>Available series indicate that a subset of partial-thickness tears ultimately requires operative treatment. Surgically treated patients consistently demonstrated greater than 50% tendon thickness involvement, with a threshold exceeding 55% reported as predictive of subsequent operative management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r24">[24]</xref> Other series classified partial tears by ultrasonographic length (Grade I &#x0003c;10 mm, Grade II 10&#x02013;20 mm, Grade III &#x0003e;20 mm), with operative conversion highest in Grade III injuries (38.5%) and lowest in Grade I lesions (6.6%), supporting the concept that tear length may help anticipate failure of conservative management.</p>
        <p>Early clinical outcomes following surgical management of partial tears have generally been favorable in short-term follow-up cohorts. However, the literature remains limited, and additional studies are necessary to refine indications, optimal timing, and comparative effectiveness of different surgical techniques.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Complete Ruptures</bold>
</p>
        <p>Definitive management of complete patellar tendon rupture involves operative repair to reestablish extensor mechanism continuity, thereby restoring active knee extension and functional ambulation. Most complete ruptures occur at or near the tendon&#x02019;s proximal attachment to the inferior patellar pole, although midsubstance disruptions are also observed. Midsubstance tears may present greater technical challenges due to inferior tissue quality and less reliable suture purchase. Surgical repair is generally indicated regardless of age or activity level and should be performed promptly once the diagnosis is confirmed to optimize biological healing and facilitate rehabilitation.</p>
        <p>A variety of fixation strategies have been described. Augmentation approaches vary according to tissue quality, tear pattern, and surgeon preference.</p>
        <p>Transosseous tunnel fixation represents the most widely used method of primary repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r26">[26]</xref> This technique typically involves creating 2 to 4 tunnels through the patella, through which high-strength sutures are passed and securely tied to restore the tendon to its footprint. Construct integrity depends largely on the tendon&#x02013;suture interface, making meticulous suture placement essential. Commonly employed configurations include Mason&#x02013;Allen, Bunnell, and Krackow patterns.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r28">[28]</xref> The Krackow technique is frequently utilized due to its locking running configuration and robust tendon purchase. Advanced imaging studies indicate that, despite multiple suture passes, this method does not substantially compromise tendon vascularity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>Suture anchor fixation offers an alternative to transosseous patellar tunnels, with primary fixation achieved using anchors placed at the inferior patellar pole.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r30">[30]</xref> Advantages of this approach include limited surgical exposure, reduced risk of iatrogenic violation of patellar articular cartilage, shorter operative duration, and more anatomic restoration of the native tendon footprint. A modification employing cortical buttons in place of anchors has been described to enhance construct strength.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>Suture-tape fixation with knotless anchors demonstrates load-to-failure comparable to transosseous suture constructs while reducing gap formation under load. Clinically, knotless anchor techniques may allow a less invasive approach with smaller incisions, reduced soft-tissue dissection, and shorter operative time.</p>
        <p>Repair augmentation is indicated in cases of compromised tendon quality, including fraying at tear margins or persistent gapping that threatens fixation stability. Augmentation may be achieved using high-strength suture constructs, cerclage cable wire, or biologic reinforcement with autograft or allograft tissue. The objective of augmentation is to reduce strain across the repair, limit gap formation, and potentially facilitate earlier progression of knee motion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>Hamstring autograft augmentation is commonly employed, typically using a doubled graft incorporated into the repair by securing it within a distal patellar pole socket and suturing it to the native tendon. In revision cases or severe tissue compromise, circumferential cerclage wiring around the patella with fixation to the tibial tubercle may provide additional protection. Transosseous repair reinforced with woven surgical mesh has also been reported to enhance resistance to cyclic gap formation following repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>Internal bracing has emerged as an adjunctive strategy for acute patellar tendon repair, providing supplemental load-sharing support to the primary repair. This technique typically uses high-strength suture tape secured with knotless anchors to enhance construct stability and protect the repair during early rehabilitation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r34">[34]</xref> A technical variation incorporates patellar bone tunnels combined with tibial cortical button fixation, with the suture tape whipstitched through the tendon to reinforce the repair construct.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r35">[35]</xref> Potential advantages of internal bracing include stronger fixation, earlier initiation of postoperative mobilization, and more rapid functional recovery.</p>
        <p>Biologic and regenerative augmentation strategies include platelet-rich plasma (PRP) and engineered tissue constructs. These adjuncts&#x000a0;aim to promote tendon healing and restore tissue quality after injury.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r36">[36]</xref>&#x000a0;The clinical efficacy of PRP remains uncertain in acute patellar tendon rupture, and isolated reports have documented tendon rupture following PRP treatment for chronic tendinopathy.</p>
        <p>Tissue-engineering techniques using collagen- and fibrin-based scaffolds have demonstrated the ability to approach native tendon tensile strength in experimental models. However, longer-term clinical experience with synthetic scaffolds has been limited by complications such as implant failure and synovitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r37">[37]</xref> Currently available biomaterials and scaffold constructs have not consistently replicated the full mechanical, structural, and biological properties of native tendon, highlighting the need for further development and rigorous clinical evaluation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis of patellar tendon rupture includes quadriceps tendon rupture, patellar fracture, and tibial tubercle avulsion fracture. These conditions may present with loss of active knee extension and anterior knee pain, yet differ in anatomical location, imaging findings, and definitive management.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s10" sec-type="Staging">
        <title>Staging</title>
        <p>The Popkin and Golman classification stratifies partial patellar tendon tears according to anterior&#x02013;posterior tendon thickness and the proportion of tendon involvement. The grading system is as follows:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Grade 1: No demonstrable tear; tendon thickness&#x000a0;less than 8 mm. Management is nonoperative and includes bracing, physiotherapy, and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Grade 2: Partial-thickness tear involving&#x000a0;less than 25% of tendon thickness. Treatment remains conservative with bracing, physiotherapy, and NSAIDs, with possible adjunctive use of PRP and ESWT.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Grade 3: Tear involving 25% to 50% of tendon thickness. Management is nonoperative with bracing, physiotherapy, and NSAIDs, supplemented by adjuncts such as PRP and ESWT; dry needling may be considered where appropriate.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Grade 4: High-grade partial tear involving over 50% of tendon thickness. Initial management is conservative with bracing, physiotherapy, NSAIDs, and adjunctive therapies (PRP, ESWT, dry needling); surgical intervention should be considered if symptoms persist despite approximately 6 months of structured nonoperative treatment.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The system has been proposed as a practical adjunct to guide treatment decisions. Persistent high-grade lesions, particularly grade 4 tears that remain symptomatic despite approximately 6 months of structured nonoperative management, warrant consideration of operative d&#x000e9;bridement, with repair performed when indicated.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Timely surgical repair of patellar tendon rupture is typically associated with favorable-to-excellent functional outcomes.&#x000a0;Suboptimal recovery, complications, and repair failure are frequently related to diagnostic and therapeutic&#x000a0;delays or errors in surgical technique.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications associated with patellar tendon rupture include rerupture, persistent extensor mechanism weakness, extensor lag with incomplete knee extension, knee stiffness, quadriceps atrophy, and infection. Mitigation requires meticulous surgical technique, appropriate fixation, structured rehabilitation emphasizing early controlled motion and quadriceps activation, and vigilant postoperative monitoring for wound complications.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s13" sec-type="Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care">
        <title>Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care</title>
        <p>The postoperative rehabilitation protocol varies according to surgeon preference.&#x000a0;The following serves as a general guideline.</p>
        <p>During weeks 0 through 2, the primary goal is the protection of the surgical tendon repair. Weight-bearing as tolerated is permitted with crutches and a knee brace locked in full extension. Range-of-motion (ROM) allowances are determined by the operating surgeon based on repair quality.</p>
        <p>During weeks 2 through 6, the goal is continued protection of the repair and normalization of gait with crutches and a knee brace. Weight-bearing as tolerated continues with crutches and the brace locked in full extension. Passive ROM from 0&#x000b0; to 90&#x000b0; of knee flexion is initiated, without active quadriceps extension. ROM progression remains at the discretion of the operating surgeon according to repair integrity.</p>
        <p>Weeks 6 through 12 focus on normalization of gait on flat surfaces, progressive weaning from crutches, gradual knee brace adjustment to allow flexion, and initiation of active quadriceps contraction. Weight-bearing is advanced cautiously, avoiding flexion beyond 70&#x000b0;, while active ROM exercises are performed. Progressive strengthening includes&#x000a0;physical therapy modalities,&#x000a0;such as light squats, leg presses, and core exercises.</p>
        <p>Weeks 12 through 16 aim to achieve a normalized gait on all surfaces without a brace, full ROM, single-leg stance with good control, and squats to 70&#x000b0; of knee flexion with proper technique. Nonimpact balance and proprioceptive drills are initiated, while quadriceps and core strengthening exercises continue.</p>
        <p>Beyond 16 weeks, rehabilitation targets restoration of quadriceps control and pain-free performance during sport- or work-specific movements, including impact activities. Return-to-sport criteria include dynamic neuromuscular control during multiplane activities without pain or swelling.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Novel Rehabilitation Modalities</bold>
</p>
        <p>Blood flow restriction training (BFRT) facilitates hypertrophy and strength gains using low loads. Tendon-related outcomes in healthy cohorts are variable but generally comparable to high-load or conventional low-load resistance training. Tendon-specific responses remain heterogeneous in rehabilitation settings.&#x000a0;However, several studies report clinically meaningful improvements in pain, strength, performance, and condition-specific self-reported function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r39">[39]</xref></p>
        <p>The current evidence base for&#x000a0;blood flow restriction training in healthy and pathological tendons is limited. Early data indicate potential clinical and tendon-specific benefits, yet further high-quality research is required before definitive rehabilitation recommendations can be established.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26730.r40">[40]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s14" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Patient education should emphasize the nature of the surgical procedure and the critical importance of strict adherence to postoperative follow-up, particularly structured physical therapy. Nonoperative management similarly requires disciplined compliance with prescribed therapeutic exercises. Laxity in performing scheduled or home-based rehabilitation can result in permanent functional deficits. Realistic expectations must account for age, preinjury conditioning, and the demands of work and daily activities. Optimal outcomes are achievable with successful surgical intervention combined with consistent adherence to postoperative care and rehabilitation protocols.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Diagnosis and management of patellar tendon rupture are most effective when delivered through an interprofessional pathway involving orthopedic clinicians, physiotherapists, and orthopedic nursing staff. Management decisions should be guided by the extent of tendon disruption and the integrity of the extensor mechanism. Complete ruptures require operative repair because functional impairment from extensor mechanism failure is substantial. Early intervention for acute ruptures is strongly recommended to optimize healing, facilitate rehabilitation, and reduce the likelihood that delayed presentation will necessitate complex reconstruction.</p>
        <p>Nonoperative care is generally reserved for partial-thickness tears in which active extension is preserved. Conservative management includes bracing, structured physiotherapy, and careful clinical reassessment to detect failure of nonoperative treatment. Orthopedic nurses provide critical support in both pathways by coordinating analgesia and medications, monitoring wounds and complications, reinforcing bracing and mobilization instructions, tracking functional progress, and promptly communicating clinical changes to the treating team.</p>
        <p>All patients should follow a structured rehabilitation program to restore ROM, quadriceps strength, and functional capacity. Outcomes for most patients are favorable with timely diagnosis, appropriate surgical decision-making, and coordinated rehabilitation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26730.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
